and \code{\link[=emoji_unicode_version]{emoji_unicode_version()}} for the Unicode version on its own.

\strong{The catalogue moved in 0.6.0, so results from 0.5.0 and 0.6.0 differ, and
this row is how to tell which you have.} The catalogue is now Unicode's
\code{emoji-test.txt} rather than the table inside \pkg{emoji}, which takes it
from Emoji 16.0 to 18.0 and makes 182 more emoji detectable. In the other
direction, a skin-tone swatch standing alone and a regional-indicator pair
that is no country stopped being emoji occurrences; \code{\link[=emoji_summary]{emoji_summary()}}
counts both. A corpus re-run under 0.6.0 can therefore report more emoji
or fewer, and \code{unicode_emoji} is the column that says why.

\code{n_emoji} counts \strong{rows of the reference table}, which are spellings, not
distinct emoji. With the Emoji 18.0 catalogue it is 5244, and those 5244
rows carry only 3972 distinct code-point keys, because an emoji whose
presentation can be selected appears both with and without \code{U+FE0F}. A
methods section reporting "5244 emoji" therefore overstates the vocabulary
by the 1272 duplicate spellings; \code{length(unique(emoji_properties()$key))}
is the count of distinct emoji, nine of which are the skin-tone and hair
components, which are not emoji on their own. 212 of the 5244 spellings
are not detectable in text as written under the default \code{presentation}
(see \link{emoji_sentiment_lexicon} for why, and \code{\link[=emoji_summary]{emoji_summary()}} for
\code{presentation = "any"}). No \emph{emoji} is lost to that: every one of the 3963
keys that is not a component is reachable through at least one detectable
spelling. The two lexicon strings count their tables' rows the same way.
